---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

// Plain-language privacy policy. Every statement here describes how the site
// actually runs; when the deployment changes (deploy/, the Caddyfile, a new
// third-party service), this page changes with it. The effective date is the
// date of the last substantive edit.
const MAIL = "corrections@chinovalley.today";
const EFFECTIVE = "November 10, 2026";
---

<Base
	title="Privacy — Chino Valley Today"
	description="What Chino Valley Today collects about readers (almost nothing), what our hosting providers see, and how to reach us."
>
	<header class="legalhead">
		<div class="shell legalhead__inner">
			<h1 class="legalhead__title">Privacy</h1>
			<p class="legalhead__date mark">Effective {EFFECTIVE}</p>
		</div>
	</header>

	<div class="reading body prose">
		<p>
			Chino Valley Today is a local news site about Chino and Chino Hills. It
			collects very little about its readers. This page explains what is
			collected and who collects it.
		</p>

		<h2>The short version</h2>
		<ul>
			<li>No accounts, no sign-up, no newsletter list.</li>
			<li>No advertising, no tracking across sites, and no cookies of our own.</li>
			<li>
				Cloudflare, our network provider, counts page views without cookies
				and handles technical request data such as IP addresses.
			</li>
			<li>If you email us, we keep your message and address so we can reply.</li>
			<li>We do not sell personal information or share it for advertising.</li>
		</ul>

		<h2>When you read the site</h2>
		<p>
			Pages are plain HTML, and the fonts come from our own server. Reading a
			page contacts only us and Cloudflare, our network provider.
		</p>
		<p>
			Each page loads a small Cloudflare Web Analytics script so we can see
			which pages people read. It reports the page address, the page that
			referred you, how long the page took to load, and general details such
			as your browser type and country. It sets no cookies, stores nothing in
			your browser, and does not follow you from site to site. The site
			responds the same whether or not your browser sends a Do Not Track
			signal. In these statistics we see totals, never one reader's history.
			If your browser blocks the script, the site works the same.
		</p>
		<p>
			Some pages load one or two more small scripts: the podcast player, and
			one Cloudflare uses to keep our email address from spam harvesters.
		</p>
		<p>
			Our web server keeps no log of ordinary page requests. When a request
			fails, for example for a page that does not exist, the server records
			it in its system log: the page requested, the time, the headers your
			browser sent (such as its user agent and the referring page), and the
			IP address the request came from. That is usually Cloudflare's address,
			though the entry can also include yours as Cloudflare passes it along.
			We use these entries to keep the site running and investigate abuse, or
			when the law requires. The system log deletes its oldest entries as it
			fills, which can take several months.
		</p>
		<p>
			Traffic reaches us through Cloudflare, which provides the site's
			encryption and protects it from attacks. To do that it processes the
			same request information, including your IP address, and shows us
			traffic statistics, such as the most requested pages and the IP
			addresses sending the most requests. It may set a strictly necessary
			security cookie when it needs to check that a request is not an attack.
			See the
			<a
				class="headline-link"
				href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/"
				rel="noopener noreferrer">Cloudflare privacy policy</a
			>.
		</p>
		<p>
			Requests for the RSS feed and podcast episodes are handled the same way.
			Feed readers, podcast apps, and sites we link to have their own privacy
			practices.
		</p>

		<h2>When you email us</h2>
		<p>
			Mail to <a href={`mailto:${MAIL}`}>{MAIL}</a> is forwarded by Cloudflare
			Email Routing to the editor's inbox, which a third-party email provider
			hosts. We use your message and address to answer you and to make any
			correction you report. We do not add you to any list, and we will not
			publish your name or message without your permission.
		</p>

		<h2>People named in the record</h2>
		<p>
			The site reports from public records: agendas, minutes, meeting video,
			license reports, and agency releases. Those records sometimes name
			people. Our editorial rules limit when a private individual is named and
			do not allow naming minors. Agency alerts appear as the agency's own
			headline with a link to its page. To ask about an item that names you,
			email <a href={`mailto:${MAIL}`}>{MAIL}</a>.
		</p>

		<h2>Children</h2>
		<p>
			The site is not directed to children under 13, and we do not knowingly
			collect personal information from them.
		</p>

		<h2>Your choices and rights</h2>
		<p>
			You can read the whole site without an account and without giving us
			your name or email address. To have your emails to us deleted, or to ask
			what we hold about you, email
			<a href={`mailto:${MAIL}`}>{MAIL}</a>. We aim to answer within 30 days.
			We cannot look up or remove individual entries in technical logs. The
			California Consumer Privacy Act applies to businesses larger than this
			site, and we do not sell or share personal information as that law
			defines those terms.
		</p>

		<h2>Changes</h2>
		<p>
			When this policy changes, we update this page and the effective date at
			the top.
		</p>
	</div>
</Base>

<style>
	.legalhead {
		background: var(--crate);
		color: #fff;
		border-bottom: 6px solid var(--placard);
	}
	.legalhead__inner {
		padding-block: 1.9rem 2.2rem;
		max-width: 44rem;
	}
	.legalhead__title {
		margin: 0 0 0.6rem;
		font-size: clamp(1.55rem, 1.2rem + 2vw, 2.5rem);
		line-height: 1.16;
		font-weight: 600;
	}
	.legalhead__date {
		margin: 0;
		font-size: 0.78rem;
	}
	.body {
		padding-block: 2.4rem 1rem;
	}
</style>
